<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    What a long press on the app icon offers.

    Two shortcuts, and they differ in what they can promise. "Restart" opens the sheet rather than asking for a
    target of its own, because which of the six this phone will do at this moment depends on whether root or a
    Shizuku shell answers right now - a probe, not a constant - and a shortcut hard-coded to "reboot to
    recovery" would be a button that fails silently on a phone with neither.

    "Soft restart" is the one target asked for directly: it restarts the Android userspace and leaves the
    kernel alone, so a tap that turns out to be a mistake costs fifteen seconds, and it is the step a root run
    usually ends with. It still answers to the same probe - with no root the daemon cannot restart anything, so
    the app opens the sheet that says so. The four targets that leave Android stay behind the sheet's own
    confirmation.

    Android's own power menu already restarts the phone, which is why there is no shortcut for a plain reboot:
    what these add is everything the sheet offers that the system's menu does not.
-->
<shortcuts xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!--
        `android:targetPackage` is the build's applicationId, which in this fork is deliberately not the
        Java package the action strings above name: only the former decides which app the launcher opens,
        and the two installs can both be present. RestartShortcutTest reads this attribute against
        BuildConfig.APPLICATION_ID, so a rename here cannot go unnoticed.
    -->
    <shortcut
        android:shortcutId="restart_options"
        android:enabled="true"
        android:icon="@drawable/ic_shortcut_restart"
        android:shortcutShortLabel="@string/shortcut_restart_short"
        android:shortcutLongLabel="@string/reboot_sheet_title">
        <intent
            android:action="ctrl.mietze.veyraroot.action.RESTART_OPTIONS"
            android:targetPackage="dev.rushiranpise.rmgnext"
            android:targetClass="ctrl.mietze.veyraroot.MainActivity" />
    </shortcut>

    <!--
        The same window as the shortcut above, and deliberately: the direct action runs in the activity the app
        already has rather than in a second one added for it. What it costs is a moment of the app's window
        before the restart - which is also the only feedback the probe can give while it runs - and what it
        saves is a second exported entry point that any app on the phone could poke.

        The icon is the sheet's, because a second glyph would have to be drawn by hand: the platform's own icon
        set is a Compose API and a shortcut icon has to be a drawable. The labels are what tell the two apart.
    -->
    <shortcut
        android:shortcutId="soft_restart"
        android:enabled="true"
        android:icon="@drawable/ic_shortcut_restart"
        android:shortcutShortLabel="@string/reboot_target_soft_restart"
        android:shortcutLongLabel="@string/shortcut_soft_restart_long">
        <intent
            android:action="ctrl.mietze.veyraroot.action.SOFT_RESTART"
            android:targetPackage="dev.rushiranpise.rmgnext"
            android:targetClass="ctrl.mietze.veyraroot.MainActivity" />
    </shortcut>
</shortcuts>
